\begin{theorem}\hypertarget{tag-02RE}{}\label{night:P2.4}\label{stmt:P2.4}
\StatementRecord{night:P2.4}{proved}{02RE}{the definitions and constructions stated in the proof}

For every admissible $(3,4)$ twist, the marked map $f:X_c\to\Pp^1$ extends meromorphically over every hypothetical proper Hausdorff analytic filling of a finite parameter cover. A proper modification carries a holomorphic marked map to $\Pp^1$, and hence a proper map to $\Pp^1\times\Delta$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\begin{enumerate}[label={$\langle1\rangle$\arabic*.},leftmargin=*]
\item Take the reduced irreducible component containing the punctured family. It is closed and proper; discarding other central components preserves that family. Resolve it isomorphically over the smooth punctured family. We use analytic resolution \cite[Theorem 1.6 and the following analytic-neighborhood paragraph, preprint p.~5]{BM97}: reduced complex analytic spaces admit proper modifications by locally finite sequences of smooth-centre blowups with smooth result. It can be chosen unchanged over the nonsingular locus. Properness makes the neighborhoods of the central fibre relatively compact, so the stated analytic hypotheses hold. Write $p:Z\to\Delta$ for the smooth result.

\item The determinant calculation in Part~5 gives $K_{X_c}^{-2}=f^*\OO(1)$. Connected compact fibres give $f_*\OO_X=\OO_{\Pp^1}$, so $h^0(K_{X_c}^{-2})=2$. Let $L=\omega_{Z/\Delta}^{-2}$. Grauert's coherence theorem \cite[Hauptsatz I]{Grauert60} makes $p_*L$ coherent. Multiplication by the parameter is injective on $L$ and on $p_*L$. A finitely generated torsion-free module over $\C\{r\}$ is free, so this direct image has rank two. On the punctured disc, $p$ is a submersion, $L$ is flat and $h^0=2$ is constant. Grauert's base-change theorem \cite[\S7, Satz 5]{Grauert60} identifies its frame with the complete fibrewise linear system. Evaluation gives a meromorphic map $f':Z\dashrightarrow\Pp^1$ whose restriction is $f$ followed by a projective linear transformation on each noncentral fibre.

\item Blowing up the base ideal and resolving its graph gives a proper holomorphic map $(f',p)$ to $\Pp^1\times\Delta$. The rank-drop locus is analytic. Its image is analytic by the proper-image theorem \cite[\S7, Satz 1]{Grauert60}. Away from the central parameter the critical image has exactly three points, labelled by the multiplicity-three fibre, multiplicity-four fibre and cusp. Each traces a nonvertical analytic curve of degree one over the punctured disc. The normalization of its closure gives a meromorphic section of $\Pp^1$ over the disc. The limits may coincide, but the three meromorphic sections remain distinct.

\item The unique cross-ratio transformation taking these sections to $0,1,\infty$ has meromorphic coefficients. Composing with $f'$ gives the meromorphic marked map. Its coefficients have finite-order poles. Resolving its graph gives the asserted holomorphic map; the modifications are proper and preserve the punctured family. This proves the needed extension without making a graph-closure assertion for an arbitrary map from a punctured disc.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proof}
